    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae, commonly used in preparation of food and beverage by humans, has been historically been considered to be a harmless fungus. Increasing reports in recent years, however, have cited S. cerevisiae as the cause of infection in both immunocompromised and healthy human patients. Accordingly, a major outstanding question is, what are the genetic adaptations that have resulted in increased virulence? In this project, we examined several candidate genes that may be guiding this pathogenesis by comparing allele-specific fitness levels of a clinical, pathogenic strain of S. cerevisiae to a nonclinical lab strain in vitro. The clinical strain alleles for SRX1, ATG33, and FLO11 exhibited a significant increase in growth at both 30 ̊ C and 37 ̊ C. In addition, the clinical strain alleles for CYT2 and LHS1 exhibited significantly increased fitness at 37 ̊ C relative to 30 ̊ C. These results provide evidence that these genes may be associated with increased pathogenicity, however, we plan to study these strains in vivo for further comparison.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
